-- Expectations that are MEANT TO FAIL: each asserts the answer of a reading of the statute that the fork register
-- (NOTES.md section 3, entries F27 to F34, found in pass 2) considered and rejected. The encoding takes the other reading,
-- so every assertion here must fail. check.sh counts them (expected_failed in check.sh: 14), as it counts those of
-- tests-red.l4 (F1 to F25, from pass 1). If one of them ever passes, the encoding has changed its reading.

§ `Tests that are meant to fail: readings considered and rejected in pass 2`

§§ `Fixtures`

`a detachable cabin standing on the ground` MEANS `A thing of property` WITH
    `it is tangible` IS TRUE, `it is ground` IS FALSE, `it is built on ground` IS TRUE, `it is planted in ground` IS FALSE, `it is attached to ground permanently` IS FALSE, `it is a detachable fixture` IS TRUE

`use for guarding only` MEANS `A holding of land` WITH
    `it is possession in the ordinary sense` IS FALSE, `it is use, possession or enjoyment` IS TRUE, `it is as an employee or official only` IS FALSE, `it is for supervision or guarding only` IS TRUE, `it is use` IS FALSE, `it is possession` IS FALSE, `it is enjoyment` IS FALSE

`a rule that stopped at 00:01:00 on 2026-03-01` MEANS `A thing a repeal might be thought to revive` WITH
    `valid from` IS `the moment on` (YMD 2010 1 1) `at` 0 `:` 0 `:` 0
    `valid until` IS JUST (`the moment on` (YMD 2026 3 1) `at` 0 `:` 1 `:` 0)

`the 2000 wording` MEANS `A version of an enactment's wording` WITH `in force from` IS YMD 2000 1 1, `wording` IS "the wording as first enacted"
`the 2010 wording` MEANS `A version of an enactment's wording` WITH `in force from` IS YMD 2010 1 1, `wording` IS "the wording with an added provision"

§§ `F27: the exception "except detachable fixtures" reaches every limb`

-- Rejected: a detachable thing built on the ground is not immovable. Taken: the exception qualifies only the last limb, the things attached.
#ASSERT NOT `s 3 "immovables" — the thing is immovable:` `a detachable cabin standing on the ground`
#ASSERT NOT `Ordinance s 1 "immovables" — the thing is immovable, as the Ordinance now defines it:` `a detachable cabin standing on the ground`

§§ `F28: supervision or guarding is a further limb of what "possession" includes`

-- Rejected: use only for supervision or guarding is possession. Taken: neither exclusion may hold.
#ASSERT `s 3 "possession" — the holding of land is possession:` `use for guarding only`
#ASSERT `Ordinance s 1 "possession" — the holding of land is possession:` (`A holding of land under the Ordinance` WITH `possession in the ordinary sense` IS FALSE, `use, residence, possession or enjoyment` IS TRUE, `as an employee or official only` IS FALSE, `for supervision or guarding only` IS TRUE, `use` IS FALSE, `residence` IS FALSE, `possession` IS FALSE, `enjoyment` IS FALSE)

§§ `F29: the note's "miles" are statute miles, and the outer edge is outside the strip`

-- Rejected: twelve statute miles are 10.43 nautical miles, so a point 11 nautical miles out is outside the strip.
#ASSERT NOT `Ordinance s 1 "coastal waters" — the note's strip, read as the Law's twelve nautical miles:` (`A point at sea` WITH `it is in the open sea along the coast of the State` IS TRUE, `its distance seaward from the low-water point on the shore, in nautical miles` IS 11)

-- Rejected: a strip "of a width of twelve miles" stops short of its outer edge, so a point exactly twelve nautical miles out is outside.
#ASSERT NOT `s 3 "coastal waters" — the point is within the coastal waters:` (`A point at sea` WITH `it is in the open sea along the coast of the State` IS TRUE, `its distance seaward from the low-water point on the shore, in nautical miles` IS 12)

§§ `F30: "several" (מספר)`

-- Rejected for s 16: a body of one member is "a body of several members". Taken: at least two.
#ASSERT `s 16 — a power to appoint a body of` 1 `members covers` `a chairman for the body` `, the chairman being a member:` TRUE `, for` `a matter nothing displaces`

-- Rejected for s 20 (pass 1's rule takes one person as enough, with its own passing test in tests-law.l4): by the reading
-- taken for s 16, "a number of persons" is at least two, so an act imposed on one person is a case the section does not speak of.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 20 — an act imposed on` 1 `persons was validly done when` 1 `of them did it` BECAUSE "s 20 speaks of an act imposed on several persons and done by some of them; the numbers given do not fit that"

§§ `F31: day-level boundaries of ss 22(1) and 25`

-- Rejected: a version "in force from" a day does not yet apply on that day itself. Taken: it applies from that day.
#ASSERT `s 25 — the wording in force on` (YMD 2010 1 1) `among` (LIST `the 2000 wording`, `the 2010 wording`) EQUALS JUST `the 2000 wording`

-- Rejected: a thing valid until the very moment a repeal takes effect no longer had validity at that hour. Taken: s 8, "until" includes its end.
#ASSERT NOT `s 22(1) — the thing had validity at the hour a repeal commencing on` (YMD 2026 3 1) `took effect:` `a rule that stopped at 00:01:00 on 2026-03-01`

§§ `F32: the exclusion in "master" belongs to the second limb only`

-- Rejected: a navigator who has control of the ship is the master. Taken: the exclusion closes the whole sentence.
#ASSERT `Ordinance s 1 "master" — the person is the master:` (`A person in relation to a ship` WITH `has control of a ship or vessel` IS TRUE, `is appointed over the ship or vessel at that time` IS FALSE, `a navigator` IS TRUE, `a harbour master` IS FALSE)

§§ `F33: the Ordinance's definitions also govern enactments given after the Law`

-- Rejected: the opening words ("every enactment hereafter given") still reach later enactments for words the Law does not define.
#ASSERT `Ordinance s 1 — the Ordinance's definitions govern` `the Ordinance entry on "person"` `in an enactment given on` (YMD 1990 1 1)

§§ `F34: the scope of a qualifier inside an entry`

-- Rejected: the exceptions in "seaman" (the master, a navigator, an apprentice) remove from the word even a person who is a seaman in the ordinary sense.
#ASSERT NOT `Ordinance s 1 "seaman" — the person is a seaman:` (`A person aboard a ship` WITH `a seaman in the ordinary sense` IS TRUE, `works on a ship or is employed on it in any capacity` IS TRUE, `the master` IS TRUE, `a navigator` IS FALSE, `an apprentice bound by a contract and properly registered` IS FALSE, `works on a ship` IS FALSE, `is employed on a ship in any capacity` IS FALSE)

-- Rejected: "in a form visible to the eye" qualifies only the last of the ways of presenting words ("every other way"), so print that is not visible is still in writing.
#ASSERT `Ordinance s 1 "in writing" — the presentation counts as writing:` (`A presentation of words or numerals` WITH `writing in the ordinary sense` IS FALSE, `presents words or numerals, or copies them` IS TRUE, `in a form visible to the eye` IS FALSE, `by printing` IS FALSE, `by lithography` IS FALSE, `by typewriting` IS FALSE, `by photography` IS FALSE)
